import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "3 John",
  display: "Third John",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, this letter is not just a simple continuation from the previous two. Arguably, they are directed to the same people, but the letters are all themed to one incredible doctrine that all churches (even still today) are to support and focus on. That doctrine is the doctrine of love. We don’t have that preached behind the pulpit nearly enough. We might preach about the love of God. We might preach about the Salvation of Christ. We might preach about the coming judgment. We will preach about grace, and we will preach about mercy, and we will preach about being a soldier of the Lord. But I don’t think I’ve ever heard a sermon preached about loving God’s people."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here is John, however, preaching about that message, and it would be wise of us to take an ear to it. This will be the third time that this theme is harped on, and it baffles me that we have three letters themed with the same message, and yet hardly ever talked about in the church. What has our faith and work become? It’s all about selfish ambition and creating a false narrative today. The goal is to get more people in seats and more money in pockets. It’s about making you feel welcomed, only to make you work for the pastor. I’m sorry if that offends the reader, but it is true. The church has lost its genuineness because the leaders have lost their love."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we want to represent the doctrine of Christ boldly and correctly, we must LIVE in the doctrine of love (at least according to John). He was VERY adamant about this message since the first letter. It is believed that these letters were written after Revelation (perhaps that’s true), and so John (at this point in life) is an old man (physically) and has learned what really matters in the faith. To him, as he gets older, he recognizes that people are not loving like they should be. They are far too caught up with growing that they forget how to love like dear children. Children are a great example of many things, but the one thing that we can know for sure about children is that they genuinely love their own family. You’d be hard-pressed to find a dear child who hates his family. Even those that are abused by their parents love their parents at a young age. It’s not until they mature that they grow to hate the hurts that their parents caused."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this last letter from John, the theme will be the same, but the characters that he addresses will be different. The goal, in the end, is to get the church (and especially the leaders) to love better towards their brothers in the faith. These are not books we can just nonchalantly skip over and assume we know everything there is to know about it. Honestly, I think John STILL didn’t know the depths of love, and he had been studying it well into his old age. He probably died from old age still preaching this same message. The key trait that he got from the Lord in ministry was the very needful ministry of love (though he was labeled as a “son of thunder” probably because he didn’t dwell in this love quite yet)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t know about you, but the older I get, the more out of love with the world and with people I have been becoming. I don’t know what that says about me. I know that my faith still has to increase, and perhaps that love will spark revival in the future, but as of right now (with how dark the world has become) loving people becomes harder and harder with age. I think that is why John is harping so strongly on this theme. It is possible to get older and to love more."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"The Elder to Gaius","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John still isn’t taking authority here. He is just calling himself “the elder.” He’s just an old man talking about what he has learned in his faith through age and time. If we are not learning to go deeper in the characteristics of our faith as we get older, we are not walking with the Lord in the way we should be. Far too many Christians today think they have learned it all. The moment you think that is the moment God will confront you. I’ve seen it happen too many times."},{"kind":"para","text":"John is focusing his letter, now, to a man named Gaius. He loves this man the same way he loves the elect lady: “in the truth.” That is how every person should love someone else. It should be in the truth. I think a lot of people don’t understand that. They think that loving someone “in the work” is what is really important, or loving someone “in the church” is all that really matters. But your love is faulty if it is not “in the truth.” The truth is Jesus Christ, as we have already mentioned several times in the last two letters. Nothing has changed for John, or for the church."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":4,"title":"The Greatest Joy","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"First, you don’t call someone you are not close to “beloved.” My assumption is that Gaius is a good friend to John. At least I would hope so if he is calling him this. Also, not to mention, he is hoping for good things to come to Gaius. You don’t wish health and prosperity to someone you hate. Not usually, anyway."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps he was sick, and that is why John is writing to him here. He is praying for a miracle in his health and that God would provide more time for Gaius. I can’t say that for sure, but based on the urgency of this letter, I would assume this to be the case."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It should be a desire for every child of God to be thought of as a good testimony. People were coming to John quite frequently, it seems, to talk about how good and faithful this Gaius man was. Furthermore, we are assured that it was John who led this man to the Lord:"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Gaius was like a child of John’s. When someone comes to know Christ because of your witness to them, they become like one of your children. They should anyway. I see that forsaken in modern ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"The moment a man comes to know Christ, that man is passed around the church to be taught by different people. They are not the child of the preacher. The preacher doesn’t have time for more children. Yet the preacher keeps having them and giving them away to the brothers of the church instead of raising up the child himself."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it.","ref":"Proverbs 22:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"I’m speaking spiritually, of course. It’s no different than a wayward father. My daughters have an eleven-year age gap between them. That was not intentional; it was just the way the Lord wanted it (I guess?). Because of this, there is a lot I know my daughter is fully capable of doing to help take care of my youngest. However, I don’t expect her to raise her sister. That would be very foolish and immature of me to think and do. Yet, that is exactly how the church is run today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Pastors see many people come to know the Lord through their own preaching and soul-winning, and instead of raising that person up in the way they should go, they are just passed off to a brother in Christ who can do it for them. How sad and pathetic a leader must be to not take care of his own:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But if any provide not for his own, and specially for those of his own house, he hath denied the faith, and is worse than an infidel.","ref":"1 Timothy 5:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is not just about the family. This is about the church. Paul even illustrates this about a leader in the church after giving off a list of prerequisites for being a church leader:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"One that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all gravity; (For if a man know not how to rule his own house, how shall he take care of the church of God?)","ref":"1 Timothy 3:4-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why would that matter? You’d think those two things should be separate, but they are not, especially when you read the letter to the Ephesians. The church is also a family; it’s an extension of your family. They are not disattached. If you shun your own married family or have rules and stipulations for them, you will not treat your church family any better. If you treat your own church family better than the one that God has gifted you with through marriage, you are directly going against His commands to you. You cannot love your church family more than your married one. The example starts in your own home first. That is why it’s required that a pastor be married; he sets the example at home first. A church should be able to tell if a man is worthy to lead them based on how he leads his family."},{"kind":"para","text":"John (I assume) had a good family structure and dynamic. He loved his children. He loved his family. He loved watching them grow and being a part of the process. He had great joy in it, not just in having more kids, but in watching the kids he already had grow, learn, and experience life (in Christ). A man who has children (spiritually in the church) but doesn’t desire to watch them grow or teach them further is a false preacher and a wayward father. He does not belong in leadership; that is all I will say."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":8,"title":"A Good Faith","entries":[{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This Gaius fellow had a strong testimony. He treated the men of faith with love and compassion, and he treated strangers in the same manner. There weren’t many people who had a negative thing to say about this man. Surely, there were some who didn’t like him. There were probably some outside of the faith who hated him because of his faith. But he was a good example of a man who loved people. He was a good example of what John has been preaching about in these last three letters. He is exactly what John wanted to see in the rest of the churches."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is nothing greater than to hear that your children are following orders and doing what is right. There’s nothing better than to see your children following your footsteps with sincerity and joy. There’s nothing better than that because this world trains children to be the opposite of this. We should not want more calloused, cold, and cruel people in this world. We should want more loving, life-giving, and light-shining people to walk the earth. When God makes this Earth new, that will be all that is here. But that hasn’t come yet. Until it does, we must train up our spiritual children so that they don’t add to the darkness, but so that they illuminate the walk of truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"Gaius was a good example of this working. John didn’t preach anything that would be impossible for people to follow. Gaius proved that."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Gentiles, in this case, were people who did not believe in the Lord. Gaius has seen strangers come in and has judged them correctly for their faith. He let the people in who were genuine and shunned those who were not. He kept himself pure, holy, and happy by making the right calls for who he allowed in his presence. This is something we should all take into consideration."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think a lot of Christians are miserable today because they spend a lot of time with people at CNN or Fox News. They spend a lot of time with politicians and worldly affairs. Those things will just keep you in misery. It’s best to shun those things and live ignorantly towards them sometimes in order to walk worthy with the Lord. You will not find a joyful walk when you have a lot of stressful and evil voices screaming at you all the time about the end of the world. Joy in the Lord can only be met after you have ditched the shouts of the world. Unplug from the lies, and plug into the truth."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Being able to love people of the faith is a very important work. Everyone, however, seems to be looking out for themselves. A lot of churches are stuck on what they get out of it and how to get more people to join their own cause. Rather than just loving people who love the Lord and simply sitting with them, we have to keep our hands moving in service. Show me a minister who “loves” his congregation but doesn’t expect anything from them. Real love doesn’t expect anything in return."},{"kind":"para","text":"We can deepen our love when we agree with one another, but you can still love people you don’t agree with. There are only two types of people who are disagreeable. One is those who seriously just don’t want to see the truth and are willing to reject the truth because of it (they are people you probably shouldn’t be spending too much time with). The other are people who are just ignorant to the truth because they have never experienced it for themselves (probably thankfully because some things that Christians experience are a lot for them to handle, so God protects others from having to go through it too). Those kinds of people need a wake-up call that only comes from experiencing the pain that they are trying to believe doesn’t exist."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, a child of God (pain or not) should know how to love their own faithfully whether people are ignorant or not. Gaius was a man with health issues (possibly) and he was a man that was still faithful even through his trials. Perhaps not in the way he wanted to be, but certainly in the way that he was able to love. That was all John hoped for anyway."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"title":"The Preeminence","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have an example of the opposite of faithful love. Diotrephes was in the church (I assume). He was a faithful figure who suddenly had a distaste for John and John’s children (faith children, that is). There must have been another letter that was written, which was shredded and rejected before it could be revealed. Diotrephes is a good definition of the current church today. There are many church leaders like this who just want to be the leaders without any sort of input from negative nancies like John. He doesn’t want to hear about the things they are doing wrong and should change. He especially (as it seems) doesn’t want to hear about how to love people better. To Diotrephes, everything he was doing was fine, and people needed to mind their own business. He was the showrunner, and he was the decision-maker. You wouldn’t believe how many people I have met who think this same way. THEY are the church owners. THEY are the visionaries. THEY are the decision-makers. What they don’t realize is THEY are also the reason for the church’s failure. When you set yourself up as the leader of a church, you also set yourself up as the church’s joke. Real leaders don’t have to tell people they are leading. Furthermore, someone once said, “A staff-run church is a staff-ruined church,” and that is one hundred percent still the case today. If WE are running the show, we will ruin the blessings. If WE take control, we rob spirits."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He was even rejecting faithful members from coming into the church. Again, this is a very real thing still today. I heard one particular preacher say that if that man had ever sinned in his life, he should be completely shunned by the church and never allowed to step foot in the doors again. I was half-tempted to walk out right there because I have sinned a great deal in my life. I don’t belong in the church according to his preaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"John, however (with far more authority than this terrible preacher), disagrees with this method. The church isn’t about YOU. It’s not about the preacher. It’s not about the pastor. It’s not even about the congregants. It’s about the Lord, and yet the leaders seem to be taking all eyes off of that to be placed on themselves (giving themselves the preeminence)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he is the head of the body, the church: who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence.","ref":"Colossians 1:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"If Christ is not center to the church, the church has already started to fall apart."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"title":"Follow the Leader","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You have a choice to make here. You can follow what is good, like Gaius and his love for the brothers, or you can follow what is evil, like Diotrephes and his desire to make everything about himself. Without outright accusing Diotrephes of not being “seen of God,” that is what John is really saying here. It’s true. Perhaps Diotrephes is a saved man. Perhaps he was given the privilege of leadership at some point, that is, of the Lord. However, his actions are not seen of God. They are evil. He has not actually seen the Lord, and the Lord is not going to look upon his evil. God will deal with his evil, but He will not give him the power to be evil. If there are leaders who do evil things, that is not given by the Lord to do. If evil leaders are being evil, it is by their own wills and desires. Evil people choose to do evil things. The same is true with Christian men. We have the choice, according to John. We can follow the lead of evil or follow the lead of good."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"title":"Shunning the Faithful","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have Demetrius. John is throwing these names around everywhere like we are supposed to know them personally. They would have been prominent names in the church, no doubt. I would assume that this name is being brought up because Diotrephes shunned him. There are a lot of people who are hurt by church leaders and shunned from the faith. Many times when they are shunned out of the church, they go unnoticed by everyone else. They just start saying, “Hey, what happened to Demetrius? Maybe something is wrong with his health?” and the conversation doesn’t go any further. If a faithful member of the church disappears, it is wise for other members of the church to check up on them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, John knows the report and is sad to give the report that Demetrius has been mistreated by the leadership of the church. Demetrius (from where John is standing) seems to have a full faith, but Diotrephes doesn’t care about the faithful. He just cares about his own power and position. Don’t be a Diotrephes. I pray that you don’t end up like a Demetrius either. I truly understand Demetrius’ hurt here. Be like Gaius, who was searching to love his brothers in the faith. Be like John, reminding people about how to activate that love. Be like Jesus, who exemplified love through his actions."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":14,"title":"Face to Face","entries":[{"start":13,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Did John keep his promise and hope to see them face to face? I don’t know. Perhaps he died before getting the chance. I believe he kept his promise. He was sincere about meeting with the saints when he said it. I believe he kept his promise in the last letter too. I don’t think these letters were written too far apart, but I do believe that there was enough time in between that John was due to visit them again. I don’t even know if it’s all geared to the same churches. Perhaps it’s not. Either way, John was serious about seeing them face to face. Ink and pen were not enough for the kind of conversation that John needed to have with these people."},{"kind":"para","text":"He was not afraid to confront Diotrephes. He was looking forward to encouraging and praying with Gaius. He was interested in loving the shunned (Demetrius). John was a man full of God’s love, full of God’s Life, and full of God’s light. He was a man that absolutely and positively LIVED in truth. He was tested and affirmed in the faith more than any of these other men. If he says it’s important to love the brothers of the faith, we should be taking that far more seriously. Three letters to attest to this. Three different examples. Plenty of witnesses. The choice is really up to us. Do we follow what is good? Or do we follow what is evil?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Following what is good will take some time and effort. Following what is evil is very easy and requires little effort. Following what is good is greeting your brothers BY NAME (even having the ability and intentionality of remembering everyone’s name). Following what is good is shunning your brothers without caring about their name. Following what is good is loving your brothers (even if they are strangers coming to you for the first time). Following what is evil is loving the world without a second thought of your brothers. If you have chosen to follow what is good, then you will prove that by meeting people face to face. If you have chosen to follow what is evil, then you don’t have to prove anything at all."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14}],"verses":{"1":"The elder unto the wellbeloved Gaius, whom I love in the truth.","2":"Beloved, I wish above all things that thou mayest prosper and be in health, even as thy soul prospereth.","3":"For I rejoiced greatly, when the brethren came and testified of the truth that is in thee, even as thou walkest in the truth.","4":"I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth.","5":"Beloved, thou doest faithfully whatsoever thou doest to the brethren, and to strangers;","6":"Which have borne witness of thy charity before the church: whom if thou bring forward on their journey after a godly sort, thou shalt do well:","7":"Because that for his name's sake they went forth, taking nothing of the Gentiles.","8":"We therefore ought to receive such, that we might be fellowhelpers to the truth.","9":"I wrote unto the church: but Diotrephes, who loveth to have the preeminence among them, receiveth us not.","10":"Wherefore, if I come, I will remember his deeds which he doeth, prating against us with malicious words: and not content therewith, neither doth he himself receive the brethren, and forbiddeth them that would, and casteth them out of the church.","11":"Beloved, follow not that which is evil, but that which is good. He that doeth good is of God: but he that doeth evil hath not seen God.","12":"Demetrius hath good report of all men, and of the truth itself: yea, and we also bear record; and ye know that our record is true.","13":"I had many things to write, but I will not with ink and pen write unto thee:","14":"But I trust I shall shortly see thee, and we shall speak face to face. Peace be to thee. Our friends salute thee. Greet the friends by name."}},
  },
};
